from owning the relationship

00:11:29.275 --> 00:11:30.790
with the customer--
- Yeah, yeah, it sucks.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.230
- Which is the key thing.

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:33.063
- [Gary] The only thing.

00:11:33.063 --> 00:11:34.880
- And that, so, with Daily
Stoic, it's the email list,

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:36.780
we sell products directly
to the consumers,

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:37.880
the margins are better.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.770
But more importantly, like
when this book came out,

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:42.530
I could send a flyer to 30,000 addresses

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:45.495
in the U.S. like that, because
I control the relationship

00:11:45.495 --> 00:11:46.328
with people.
- My big reason

00:11:46.328 --> 00:11:48.260
for writing books, somebody
asked me the other day,

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:51.660
is because I don't want to be audacious

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.660
in the way that I learn.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:55.300
- Okay.
- I can't learn

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.250
from reading this book.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.083
- [Ryan] Right.

00:11:57.083 --> 00:11:58.350
- Cannot, on the record.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:00.090
- Yeah, you're not a book guy.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:03.310
- It's just not how I know, I clearly have

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.180
reading disabilities
that I'm not aware of,

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:06.950
'cause in the '80s, we
didn't really diagnose.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:11.780
But others learn, like,
it's crazy to me that people

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.310
send me emails that they've learned

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:15.440
more in reading "Crushing It."

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.057
Literally, the email's like,

00:12:17.057 --> 00:12:18.487
"Thank you for writing 'Crushing It.'

00:12:18.487 --> 00:12:21.107
"I've watched every video
you've ever put out,

00:12:21.107 --> 00:12:23.940
"but it was 'Crushing
It' that made me do,"

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.813
and I'm like, "Motherfuck,
really, like, really?"

00:12:26.813 --> 00:12:29.190
And so, not being audacious on how one

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.520
learns or consumes is such a framework

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:32.353
of how I think about it.

00:12:32.353 --> 00:12:33.920
- Well, authors, (pen
clicks) I think we get

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.630
this sort of target blindness, right?

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:38.430
An author thinks, "I'm an
author, that's what I make,"

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:39.997
and then they're like,
"so I don't do podcasts,

00:12:39.997 --> 00:12:41.425
"or I don't do emails"--
- That's crazy,

00:12:41.425 --> 00:12:42.704
you're a communicator!
- "or I don't do social."

00:12:42.704 --> 00:12:45.542
You sell ideas, and you gotta,

00:12:45.542 --> 00:12:48.970
and I think the huge thing,
there's so many people now

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:53.510
that are "reading" that
thought they didn't like books,

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.940
because it turns out they
don't like reading books.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.170
They love audiobooks, they love
e-books, they love courses.

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:02.500
They just didn't think they like this.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:03.490
- Packaging matters.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:04.400
- Yeah, of course.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.510
I love this, this is what
I live and breathe for,

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:08.900
but I can't let my obsession--
- How many books do you read?

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.950
- A lot, hundreds a year.

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:12.890
But that's what I do,

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:13.723
that's what I'm--
- Yeah, I remember that

00:13:13.723 --> 00:13:15.210
from another conversation, that's cool.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:16.380
So, like, you constantly are,

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.207
are you the kind that
reads three books at once,

00:13:18.207 --> 00:13:19.340
and you go back and forth?

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:22.390
Or are you like, "All right,
I'm gonna read this book"?

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:24.670
- I'm always reading one book, with a pen.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:27.420
Like, I was reading a book
in the car on the way here,

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.600
and I'll finish that tonight,
I'll start another one,

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:30.433
that's me.
- It's just so obvious

00:13:30.433 --> 00:13:31.266
to me how this all plays out.

00:13:31.266 --> 00:13:32.310
Like, literally, when you just said that,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:33.757
I was like, "Oh, man,
I would be in the car

00:13:33.757 --> 00:13:35.460
"reading 800 comments."

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:36.293
- Yeah, sure.
- It's just so funny

00:13:36.293 --> 00:13:38.950
how everybody has, I
love that, I love that.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:39.990
Okay, so that's it.

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:43.587
So you just believe,
"Hey, I, myself, believe

00:13:43.587 --> 00:13:46.517
"that I'm good at synthesizing,
I'm good at hypothesizing,

00:13:46.517 --> 00:13:48.167
"I spent three fuckin' years on this,

00:13:48.167 --> 00:13:50.497
"and for the people that
consume information this way,

00:13:50.497 --> 00:13:51.600
"this should crush for them."

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.747
- I think so, and then, what
I've taken from you is, like,

00:13:53.747 --> 00:13:56.427
"Okay, so my preferred medium is books.

00:13:56.427 --> 00:13:58.977
"I get the book out, now
I have to break the book

00:13:58.977 --> 00:14:02.707
"back down into podcasts,
videos, emails, social,

00:14:02.707 --> 00:14:06.057
"because I gotta reach
back out to the people

00:14:06.057 --> 00:14:07.430
"who consume in different mediums."

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:09.410
- What else can we educate people on

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.620
