00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.370
but it has to be different.

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:57.630
- You know what's funny about that?

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.480
It's why I am so obviously
a huge sports fan.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:03.360
I have no control, and I'm completely,

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.720
I'm the Bizarro version
of myself as a sports fan.

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:06.553
I'm emotional.

00:19:06.553 --> 00:19:11.130
94.7% of my fights on Twitter have been

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.400
with Patriot fans, 94.7.

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:14.810
- There's also nothing else you would do

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:16.890
that takes four hours for yourself--

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:17.723
- [Gary] Correct.

00:19:17.723 --> 00:19:18.556
- Other than go to a Jets game.

00:19:18.556 --> 00:19:19.980
- It's the complete reset of my life.

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:20.840
- [Ryan] Yeah.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:22.840
- That Sunday resets everything.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:27.460
All 8,437 issues mean nothing
to me during those four hours.

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:30.730
- Sure, I think golf is
actually, I don't like golf,

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:33.140
but I actually get it,
'cause it's the only excuse

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:34.730
that a CEO has to be gone--

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:35.610
- I see it all the time.
- Out of the office

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:36.710
for four hours.
- That's right.

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:37.543
- It makes sense.

00:19:37.543 --> 00:19:40.240
And so you gotta, I
think people think like--

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:41.800
- I think music does it for people.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:42.890
I think video games do it.

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:44.360
I mean, I see it all the time.

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:46.620
- You gotta have your
thing, and you gotta realize

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.750
that that thing is actually
helping you be better

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:50.090
at your main thing.
- And the extension

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:52.560
of what I already do, I would
argue, when I really break

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.860
down garage sale-ing,
it isn't, now that I'm

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:59.290
going back and forth, the
thrill of rolling up on a street

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:04.190
with a fluorescent sign and
not knowing what's about to be

00:20:04.190 --> 00:20:09.190
in that person's yard, knowing
that I might find a 1954

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.398
Green Lantern cereal box,
the thrill of the hunt.

00:20:13.398 --> 00:20:14.231
- [Ryan] Mm-hm.

00:20:14.231 --> 00:20:16.540
My process, like, I
wanna try to buy the Jets

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.120
more than I actually wanna own them.

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:20.430
Like the "thrill of the hunt" is my high.

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:21.360
- Yeah, I don't think actually owning

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:22.420
the Jets would be that fun.

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:23.280
I think it's the--

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:24.240
- Well, it's gonna be super fun when

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:27.640
I beat the fuck outta the Patriots, Ryan!

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:28.650
- Yeah, of course, I'm sure you will.

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:30.370
- [Gary] What else about the book?

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:31.430
- I got something for you.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:32.263
Are we wrapping up?

00:20:32.263 --> 00:20:33.210
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:35.060
- So, I was telling you about doing--

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:37.139
- DRock, Zach, you're on Instagram Live?

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:37.972
- [Crew Member] Mm-hm.

00:20:37.972 --> 00:20:40.720
- All right, Zach, you're looking at me?

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:42.400
Thank you. Put in your
phone number right now.

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:43.780
We're about to call
one of you (clicks pen)

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:45.130
to answer a question.

00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:46.900
So start throwing your phone numbers out,

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:49.740
I'm gonna have Zach pin
one and then read it to me,

00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:51.400
and we'll call you in a minute or two.

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:52.670
- I got you the coin last
time, and I thought you--

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:53.588
- [Gary] I know.

00:20:53.588 --> 00:20:54.421
- And I thought you'd like this.

00:20:54.421 --> 00:20:56.295
This is what I wear, it's a signet ring.

00:20:56.295 --> 00:20:57.670
(ring clanks)

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.190
It's the same thing, memento mori,

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:02.130
your favorite words,
"Remember you will die."

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.110
- I love this about you.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.110
- Yeah, and then on the inside,
it's from Marcus Aurelius,

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:07.150
it says, "You could leave life right now."

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:10.210
And the idea is, this could
be the last conversation,

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:12.140
this could be the last
day you show up to work,

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:13.010
it could be the last
garage sale you go to,

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:15.170
it could be your last Jets game.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:17.910
And so, this idea of, like,
let's fuckin' do it, then,

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:19.680
let's be actually present--
- I genuinely believe

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:20.513
in that shit.

00:21:20.513 --> 00:21:21.346
- Me too.

00:21:21.346 --> 00:21:22.490
- I believe in that shit the most.

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:24.650
- People think thinking of
your mortality is morbid,

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:28.890
but I think it's the way
you embrace life fully.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:30.590
- I think about it every day.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:31.660
- Here's a crazy one, so this--

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:33.290
- [Gary] Genuinely, for real.

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:37.186
- The Stoics say when you tuck
your kids into bed at night,

00:21:37.186 --> 00:21:39.680
like, this is crazy, I
get it's controversial,

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:42.005
you go, "They might not
be here in the morning."

00:21:42.005 --> 00:21:42.838
- [Gary] I think about it every day.

00:21:42.838 --> 00:21:43.671
- I do too.

00:21:43.671 --> 00:21:46.456
- I think about every single
day having a massive tragedy.

00:21:46.456 --> 00:21:47.570
- [Ryan] Mm-hm.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:50.330
- I think about, very often,
my wife and kids in a car,

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:51.550
getting a tree landing on them

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.430
and killing them all in one shot.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:56.040
- And what does that, what do
you do with that information?

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.055
- I act as if.

00:21:58.055 --> 00:21:59.490
- Yes, yes!

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.038
So you don't hold on--

00:22:01.038 --> 00:22:02.820
- Now, wait, that doesn't
mean I'm, like, walking around

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:03.820
and looking at them every minute.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:04.860
I fuckin' work all the time!

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:06.760
- Right.
- It means that I act as if,

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:07.690
at the macro.

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:08.523
- [Ryan] Yes.

00:22:08.523 --> 00:22:09.480
- I'm grateful.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.000
It leads to gratitude.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:14.897
- Totally, and it means, "Oh,
I don't need to rush this,

00:22:14.897 --> 00:22:16.239
"I don't need to rush through this

00:22:16.239 --> 00:22:17.260
"trying to get to my phone."

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:18.890
- Is there a theory, or is there tactical,

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:20.723
or is it a mix of the two?
- [Ryan] Both.

00:22:20.723 --> 00:22:21.595
- Yeah.

00:22:21.595 --> 00:22:22.428
- [Ryan] Yeah.

00:22:22.428 --> 00:22:23.261
- And is that important
to you, based on the way,

00:22:23.261 --> 00:22:24.310
like, I think a lot about that for me.

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:25.567
- I think way too many books are like,

00:22:25.567 --> 00:22:27.470
"Here's an idea, figure it out."

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:28.303
- [Gary] Right.

00:22:28.303 --> 00:22:29.510
- And that, you gotta give an idea,

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:31.900
and then you gotta tell people
how to make that idea real.

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:33.747
- Something I've been
really trying to push is,

00:22:33.747 --> 00:22:35.090
"Watch what I'm doing."

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.130
I'm not necessarily
gonna make a white paper,

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:38.150
that's not my skill.
- That's true, true.

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:39.070
- And though we have done well,

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:41.380
we put out a video
recently, we did a deck,

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:43.660
I really want people to
watch, it's so right there!

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:44.493
- [Ryan] Yeah.

00:22:44.493 --> 00:22:45.390
- It's right there!

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:47.005
Just, why did I post that?

00:22:47.005 --> 00:22:47.838
- [Ryan] (laughs) Right.

00:22:47.838 --> 00:22:48.671
- [Gary] Like, why?

00:22:48.671 --> 00:22:49.504
- Sure, sure.

00:22:49.504 --> 00:22:50.460
- [Gary] Nothing is by accident.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:51.293
- Yeah.

00:22:51.293 --> 00:22:52.126
- [Gary] I'm not like,
"Oh, fuckin' TikTok."

00:22:52.126 --> 00:22:53.557
- Well, I think people do, they're like,

00:22:53.557 --> 00:22:55.423
"Gimme the one-, two-, three-step plan

00:22:55.423 --> 00:22:56.256
to do this."
- I hate that shit!

00:22:56.256 --> 00:22:57.560
- And it's like, if it was that easy,

00:22:57.560 --> 00:22:59.140
there'd be no value in it.
- Everybody would do it!

00:22:59.140 --> 00:22:59.973
Fuck!
- Yes, of course.

00:22:59.973 --> 00:23:00.806
- All right, Zach.

00:23:00.806 --> 00:23:01.639
(dial tone)

00:23:01.639 --> 00:23:02.472
- [Zach] 774--

00:23:02.472 --> 00:23:03.830
(keypad beeps)

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:04.663
- [Nate] Hello?

00:23:04.663 --> 00:23:05.830
- Nate?

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:06.663
- [Nate] What's up?

00:23:06.663 --> 00:23:07.662
- What's up?

00:23:07.662 --> 00:23:10.280
You're on the podcast with Ryan Holiday.

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:11.113
- Hey, man.

00:23:11.113 --> 00:23:11.946
- [Nate] Awesome, how are you guys?

00:23:11.946 --> 00:23:13.621
- We're doing well, how are you?

00:23:13.621 --> 00:23:14.680
- [Nate] I'm good, I'm good.

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:15.830
- What's your question?

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:19.750
- [Nate] What's my question?

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:21.100
Oh, I got many, man.

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:25.240
So I'm actually, I'm good
friends with Roman Atwood,

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:29.250
but I have a question on playing
video games for a living.

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:33.929
So I kinda (mumbles)
that now, trying to bring

00:23:33.929 --> 00:23:36.030
it up there again, been doing
it for the past few years,

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.530
and I just wanna see your take on that.

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:40.873
- Ryan?

00:23:40.873 --> 00:23:41.706
- [Ryan] Take on what?

00:23:41.706 --> 00:23:42.940
- The take is on, thanks
for calling, brother.

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:44.950
The take is, what do you think about

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:47.490
playing video games for a living?

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:49.430
- I mean, if you're great at--

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:50.263
- [Gary] Another phone call.

00:23:50.263 --> 00:23:51.096
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:23:51.096 --> 00:23:52.019
- [Gary] Soon-to-be-fired phone guy.

00:23:52.019 --> 00:23:52.852
(crew members laugh)
Go ahead.

00:23:52.852 --> 00:23:54.550
- Look, I think if it's what you feel like

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:55.463
you were put on this planet to do,

00:23:55.463 --> 00:23:57.430
I think it's a great
thing to do for a living.

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:01.000
I just think oftentimes,
people are attracted

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.910
to things like sports or
video games or writing

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:05.582
'cause they think it's easy.

00:24:05.582 --> 00:24:07.301
(Gary laughs)
And the point is like--

00:24:07.301 --> 00:24:08.134
- [Gary] And fun!

00:24:08.134 --> 00:24:10.110
- Yeah, the point is, actually--

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:11.190
- [Gary] It's ridiculously hard.

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:12.570
- It's not only ridiculously hard,

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:16.500
but it's so hard, only a very few amount

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:17.980
of people get to do it, right?

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:21.660
So unless you really were
put on this planet to do it

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:25.190
and you have a deep,
burning drive to do it,

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:27.190
it's not gonna happen.

00:24:27.190 --> 00:24:31.498
- The utter non-conversation around talent

00:24:31.498 --> 00:24:33.090
is fascinating to me.

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:34.820
- [Ryan] Sure, yeah.

00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:38.090
- Because it's crippling,
right, looking at somebody

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:40.983
and saying, we've, what do
you think about entitlement?

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:42.723
- I think it's awful.

00:24:42.723 --> 00:24:43.857
Like, look, I hear from people that go,

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:45.597
"I think about writing
a book," and I'm like,

00:24:45.597 --> 00:24:47.605
"Okay, don't," you know what I mean?

00:24:47.605 --> 00:24:48.438
(Gary laughs)

00:24:48.438 --> 00:24:51.027
It's like "I have to write this book.

00:24:51.027 --> 00:24:54.130
"If I don't, it will
be physically painful."

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:55.800
That's a reason to write a book, right?

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.857
It's like, "I have to start this company

00:24:58.857 --> 00:25:03.350
"because I'm so angry that x
is the current status quo."

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:04.973
You gotta have a reason.

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:08.370
- Why do you think so many people

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:11.830
live their lives so deeply in need

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:15.380
of affirmation of others,
including strangers?

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.450
- Well, I think
evolutionarily, we need it,

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:19.330
nobody wants to be outside of the tribe.

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:22.247
But people, I think a lot of this is like,

00:25:22.247 --> 00:25:24.327
"Look, I think if I'm successful,

00:25:24.327 --> 00:25:25.307
"my parents will like me.

00:25:25.307 --> 00:25:28.350
"If I have lots of Twitter
followers, I can get Dad's love."

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:29.960
You know, I think a lot of it's

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:30.793
a childhood thing--
- Or the girl,

00:25:30.793 --> 00:25:31.810
or the guy, or--

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.190
- Yeah, you're trying to
re-create where they were

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:35.930
in high school, or it's
some childhood thing

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:39.000
they're recapturing, which is
a bad reason to do anything.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:41.123
- What's up, AJ?
- [AJ] Oh, just saying hi.

00:25:41.123 --> 00:25:42.970
- Awesome, did you speak to Ryan yet?

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:44.110
- [AJ] No, I don't think we did.

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:44.943
- Good, okay, good.

00:25:44.943 --> 00:25:45.776
- [Ryan] Hey, man, how are you doing?

00:25:45.776 --> 00:25:46.609
- Hey.
- AJ Vee just walked in

00:25:46.609 --> 00:25:47.442
for a podcast piece.

00:25:47.442 --> 00:25:49.630
Anything good on VaynerSports, AJ Vee?

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:51.300
- [AJ] We got some workouts
tomorrow for our guys.

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:52.580
- There we go, putting in work.

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:53.413
- Yeah.
- I love it.

00:25:53.413 --> 00:25:54.408
- [AJ] Yeah.

00:25:54.408 --> 00:25:56.831
- (laughs) I want you to speak
to AJ real quick, 'cause--

00:25:56.831 --> 00:25:57.664
- [Ryan] Of course.

00:25:57.664 --> 00:26:00.480
- Yeah, yeah, I'm just,
there's so much interesting

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:01.313
shit going on, bro.

00:26:01.313 --> 00:26:02.146
- [Ryan] Of course.

00:26:02.146 --> 00:26:03.330
- I'm so tired of judgment.

00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:05.013
- [Ryan] I just,

00:26:05.013 --> 00:26:06.080
like, so--
- People's ability to,

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:07.840
judgment is on tilt right now.

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:09.200
- I was--
- People are obsessed

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:10.033
with judging.

00:26:10.033 --> 00:26:14.410
- I was at Stand Up New York
with James Altucher last night,

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:16.960
and I said something about
Trump, and this woman got up

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:18.187
and she was like, "I don't think it's fair

00:26:18.187 --> 00:26:19.853
"that you're doing this." I'm
not gonna make a Trump thing.

00:26:19.853 --> 00:26:24.590
What I was so surprised
by is that she gave

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:25.790
a shit about my opinion.

00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:26.993
I was like, "If you were up here,

00:26:26.993 --> 00:26:28.967
"and you were saying
something that I disagreed

00:26:28.967 --> 00:26:31.217
"with politically, you know
what I would think about it?

00:26:31.217 --> 00:26:34.520
"Nothing, because I
don't care what you do,

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:35.707
and I don't care why."

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:39.190
I don't get people who are upset

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:42.780
about Le'Veon Bell's contract negotiation.

00:26:42.780 --> 00:26:45.370
It's like, it's his life,
you know what I mean?

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.630
People have these super strong opinions

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.410
about things that don't affect them

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:52.790
because it's a way of not
having to look in the mirror

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:53.693
about your own shit.
- Politics is the one place

00:26:53.693 --> 00:26:56.110
that people make a
connection point that, A,

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:57.730
this person has control.

00:26:57.730 --> 00:26:58.563
- [Ryan] Yeah.

00:26:58.563 --> 00:26:59.690
- You know, Le'Veon Bell,
to your point, right,

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:02.220
that's why that gets to the most extreme.

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:03.053
- [Ryan] Yeah.

00:27:03.053 --> 00:27:04.250
- Right, because people
do think it has control,

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:06.600
people do think there's collateral damage.

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:07.433
- [Ryan] Yeah.

00:27:07.433 --> 00:27:08.530
- You know, and I have empathy for that.

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:11.870
I think your point's taken,
though, where you're taking it.

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:13.685
We're just judging.

00:27:13.685 --> 00:27:17.593
(laughs) Listen, in the
history of the human race,

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:23.540
anger and digging your heels
in has never been a solution,

00:27:24.190 --> 00:27:25.850
and we're just fuckin' pulling apart

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:26.740
on every fuckin' issue--

